AB  - With the modification of the Charity Law of the People′ s Republic of China and the expansion in corporate social responsibility awareness, the number of listed companies that disclose social responsibility reports has been increasing annually. Corporate philanthropy has recently attracted extensive public attention as a more direct form of corporate social responsibility. According to a report released by China Charity Alliance, mainland China received 162. 415 billion yuan of donations in 2018, including 76. 122 billion yuan donated by private and state-owned enterprises, accounting for almost half of the total amount. While corporate philanthropy is a crucial way for enterprises to fulfill their social responsibilities, disputes have been raised over donation amounts and their underlying motivation. Investors are not in favor of large donations despite the public criticizing enterprises for stingy behavior. Further, although most companies assert that corporate philanthropy enhances their image, critics still accuse firms of having impure motivations in making such donations. In terms of donation budgets, some companies act more casually while others consciously standardize the decision-making and approval process of external corporate donation behavior, even explicitly limiting annual amounts. Various donation arrangements suggest that companies may have different strategic purposes. A network of directors has been formed through the direct and indirect connections established by simultaneously serving on the same boards of directors. This network deeply affects the individual behavior of directors and the behavior of the companies they serve; this results in corresponding economic consequences. This paper examines the strategic arrangements behind corporate philanthropy actions from the perspective of board ties using empirical methods based on 2009-2016 Chinese A-share-listed firm data. The results indicate that board network centrality has a substitution effect on corporate philanthropy. The three main results of the study are as follows. 1) Firms with higher board network centrality conduct less corporate philanthropy, suggesting a substitution rather than a complementary relationship. 2) The inhibitory effect that high board network centrality poses on corporate philanthropy is more pronounced among firms with higher levels of financial risk. 3) This inhibitory effect is driven mainly by internal board members but not independent board members. This study enriches the literature on corporate philanthropy, providing a reference for future regulatory design. This paper makes two important contributions. First, it expands the research on the influence that a company′s board network position has on corporate behavior. Previous studies have looked at network position as a factor that can affect directors′ cognitive framework and have addressed several beneficial impacts that network position has on corporate decisions and outside firm characteristics. However, our results demonstrate that firms fail to fulfill their social responsibilities in accordance with their board network position and that board network position confers social capital that can be speculated upon rather than providing a passive environment for decision-makers. Moreover, firms already recognize the social capital attribute of their network position and consciously consider its costs and benefits. Diverging from previous research that posits that network position as social capital affects corporate governance and corporate value, this study found that directors′ network position can conserve company′ s social relationship expenditures to some extent, thus unveiling another channel through which directors′ network position can influence corporate value from the perspective of expenditures. Second, this study presents a new perspective on the determinants of corporate philanthropy and deepens the understanding of its underlying strategic motivations. Previous studies on strategic donations have primarily focused on company′s political connections and found that companies would make more donations to maintain their political connections. However, the high level of political uncertainty in China renders political connections vulnerable; local government transitions and mandatory resignations of board members with political backgrounds can disrupt company′s political connections. This paper discussed the more commonly influential factor of strategic donation. Additionally, it was found that when a company′s financial risk is high, its board network centrality has a more obviously inhibitory effect on its donation behavior, which further strengthens the strategic action behind corporate philanthropy. © 2022, Journal of Industrial Engineering/ Engineering Management. All Rights Reserved.
