UR  - https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/85007843921?origin=resultslist
AB  - This article examined the effects of board structure of American media companies on their performance from a stakeholder perspective. Results showed that both the level of board interlocks and the proportion of non-independent directors on the board were positively associated with corporate performance, as measured by return on assets and return on equity. Results also showed that the adoption of staggered boards had a positive effect on return on assets of media companies. However, this study did not find any relationship between board size and firm performance within media industries. © 2010 Journal of Media Business Studies.
